            "review_title": "Review at Yakiniku Yazawa Saigon",
            "review_text": "maryyeo\nHo Chi Minh City, Vietnam4531 contributions\n0\nAll the great beef and international reputation CANNOT save the poor service\nJun 2020\nI write this review with sadness, as it is such a pity that this restaurant which serves wonderful quality beef failed at the most basic service. Read the good reviews, and heard of its great reputation from Singapore (and US and Japan), we were here to try the best they had to offer - the premium omakase at 2.58mil++ VND per person. \n\nThe head-scratching service we received:\n1. When ordering, we asked the service staff what their 'premium steak cut' of the day was. The young staff didn't even know what we were asking about. After several failed attempts (using both English+Vietnamese to ask her), another experienced and English-speaking staff managed to help us ask the chef for the premium steak cut available for the day. She told us it was the Chateaubriand cut. Question: how can the service staff not be briefed on this BEFORE they started the evening service? This is standard protocol at ALL good fine dining restaurants.\n\n2. When served each course, we asked our young staff again for each item we were served, because we wanted to know what we were eating. She either ignored us or mumbled unintelligibly and we had to ask her a few times what she said. Honestly, we asked nicely.\n\n3. Eventually when she served us the course of the premium steak cut, she told us it was the tenderloin cut. We tried to clarify that her co-worker told us at the point of ordering that it was the Chateaubriand cut for the day, she said it wasn't.\n\n4. We managed to get hold of the roaming male floor manager. He confirmed for us it was the Chateaubriand cut. At that point, we were pretty stressed out by the real dodgy and confusing service we received. We asked the same floor manager for a change of staff since it was still early and many staff were available and standing around.... He simply rushedly apologized for the error in the cut served, then immediately walked away.\n\nConclusion:\nIt is just very sad and disappointing, that a multi-national fine-dining Japanese restaurant packed with numerous international guests, would fail to train their staff on the menu, on English, and on basic service. There were many chances for service recovery, but various staff managed to fail at every chance. It was also very noisy once it gets packed at 7+pm. Based on my experience here, and comparing it to other Japanese fine dining places we've been to in town, I honestly cannot recommend this restaurant for Japanese fine dining.\nRead more\nService\nFood\nWritten October 9, 2020",
